WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.892
Hi, I'm James Murphy and this is mCoding.

00:00:02.892 --> 00:00:06.160
In&nbsp;today's episode, we're talking about Python's super.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.914
super is probably Python's most misunderstood&nbsp;feature.

00:00:09.914 --> 00:00:12.000
So, today we're going to break it down.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.114
We'll cover its basic usage for single and&nbsp;multiple inheritance,

00:00:16.114 --> 00:00:21.040
go over some of the misconceptions about how it works and what&nbsp;it actually does

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.369
and then finally see a pure Python implementation of it.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:28.531
I'm also giving away&nbsp;professional licenses for PyCharm and for CLion.

00:00:28.531 --> 00:00:32.871
So, comment #pycharm or #clion if&nbsp;you're interested.

00:00:32.871 --> 00:00:36.579
Okay, here's the simplest and most common usage of super.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:39.367
We have a Base class&nbsp;that defines some function f.

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:44.021
Then we have a Derived class that derives from the Base class and&nbsp;also defines a function f.

00:00:44.021 --> 00:00:47.892
The Derived class wants to do basically the same thing the Base class does

00:00:47.892 --> 00:00:50.104
but maybe with a slight modification.

00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:54.161
Instead of copy pasting the code from the Base class which&nbsp;introduces redundant code

00:00:54.161 --> 00:00:55.976
and is also very error-prone,

00:00:55.976 --> 00:00:58.088
instead, we'll use super.

00:00:58.088 --> 00:01:02.085
Inside Derived,&nbsp;super f of x is going to call Base's f of x.

00:01:02.085 --> 00:01:06.267
The result in this case is that we see the Derived f called&nbsp;then the Base f called 

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:08.267
then the Derived f finishes.

00:01:08.273 --> 00:01:10.664
Notice that we didn't pass self into super.

00:01:10.664 --> 00:01:15.342
But&nbsp;when the base function is called, we see that self was passed as a parameter.

00:01:15.342 --> 00:01:18.252
So, you should&nbsp;not try to manually put the self parameter in.

00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:21.400
It's automatically filled in and we'll see more on&nbsp;this later.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.435
You should just pass whatever remaining positional and keyword arguments there are, in this&nbsp;case, just x.

00:01:26.435 --> 00:01:28.580
super also works just fine with class methods.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.667
And in that case, it will automatically&nbsp;pass in the class instead of an instance.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.347
Here's a more concrete example.

00:01:34.347 --> 00:01:36.918
Suppose, we want a logging&nbsp;dictionary.

00:01:36.918 --> 00:01:39.118
I want it to be usable exactly like a dictionary.

00:01:39.118 --> 00:01:44.547
But whenever I get, set, or delete a key,&nbsp;I want a logging message printed to standard out.

00:01:44.547 --> 00:01:48.501
Instead of trying to implement our own dictionary,&nbsp;we just inherit from the built-in dict

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:52.393
and then use super in the setitem, getitem and delitem methods.

00:01:52.393 --> 00:01:58.640
We print our logging message and then the super call ensures that the real&nbsp;dictionary functionality still goes through.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.560
We can then use a logging dictionary in&nbsp;much the same way that we use a normal one.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.960
Create it. Then we can set d of 0 equals "subscribe".

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.492
We can grab out the value of d of zero and&nbsp;delete it.

00:02:10.492 --> 00:02:14.447
In just a few lines of code, super allowed us to have a fully functioning dictionary

00:02:14.447 --> 00:02:17.267
that also gives us our desired logging messages.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.132
Before we move on, although I think it's extremely&nbsp;important to understand

00:02:21.132 --> 00:02:24.342
how super works and the more complicated features of it,

00:02:24.342 --> 00:02:30.369
I think that&nbsp;for 99% of you, this basic use case of super is all you will ever need.

00:02:30.369 --> 00:02:34.240
And for 1% of&nbsp;you, congrats on your incredible job security.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.829
Okay, quiz time.

00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:39.561
Suppose, we have a class A and a dot f works.

00:02:39.561 --> 00:02:44.456
Then we have a class B deriving from A that defines its own f and calls super f.

00:02:44.456 --> 00:02:48.472
Does super f necessarily call the f to find an a?

00:02:48.472 --> 00:02:50.398
Pause and think about it if you'd like.

00:02:50.398 --> 00:02:52.389
Okay, here's the&nbsp;answer.

00:02:52.389 --> 00:02:53.833
The answer is...

00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:55.137
No.

00:02:55.137 --> 00:02:59.969
In this case, although you can call f on an instance of A, A didn't define its&nbsp;own f.

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:04.165
So, when you call super f, it actually goes up to the Root's f.

00:03:04.165 --> 00:03:10.000
So, in this case, super f did not go&nbsp;to the parent of B. It went further up the chain.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.560
Okay, question two: Suppose we have a&nbsp;Root class and A inheriting from Root.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:19.108
In this case, both the Root class and A define&nbsp;their own version of f.

00:03:19.108 --> 00:03:24.653
So, my question is: Does this super f necessarily call the Root's f&nbsp;function?

00:03:24.653 --> 00:03:27.840
Once again, pause to think about it.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.467
Once again, the answer is no.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:34.083
When I run&nbsp;the example I'm about to show you, we see this A dot f.

00:03:34.083 --> 00:03:35.759
So, that code is running.

00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:40.187
But the super call ended up calling some other function B dot f.

00:03:40.187 --> 00:03:45.201
Root dot f never got printed out. So, the parent&nbsp;classes f was never called at all.

00:03:45.201 --> 00:03:49.073
This happened because A dot f wasn't called on an instance of A.

00:03:49.073 --> 00:03:52.567
It&nbsp;was the result of a super call from a child class.

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:56.963
I created a sibling class of A B which&nbsp;also inherits from Root.

00:03:56.963 --> 00:04:00.307
Then I created a C which inherits from A and B.

00:04:00.307 --> 00:04:04.160
We instantiate an&nbsp;instance of the C class and call its f function.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.098
The C class of super f calls A's f.

00:04:07.098 --> 00:04:12.026
But because&nbsp;of the multiple inheritance the super call in A doesn't go up to A's parent,

00:04:12.026 --> 00:04:14.864
instead it goes&nbsp;to A's sibling B.

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:20.755
The B class happens to not make any super call. So, the root classes f is&nbsp;never called.

00:04:20.755 --> 00:04:24.918
If we add the super call into the B class, then the Root will eventually be called.

00:04:24.918 --> 00:04:29.391
But&nbsp;notice, the super call of A still called B, not the parent Root.

00:04:29.391 --> 00:04:33.909
So, what's going on here? Why does super&nbsp;sometimes call a parent, sometimes call a sibling?

00:04:33.909 --> 00:04:37.520
It could even call different things based&nbsp;off of the class that it was called from.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.964
If I had an instance of A, this super call would&nbsp;go to Root.

00:04:40.964 --> 00:04:45.007
But from an instance of C, that same super call went to B.

00:04:45.007 --> 00:04:50.493
The answer has to do with&nbsp;what's called the class's Method Resolution Order or MRO.

00:04:50.493 --> 00:04:56.090
It's actually kind of a misnomer because it&nbsp;applies to all attribute accesses not just method lookups.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:01.472
Consider the same inheritance hierarchy&nbsp;but instead of f being a function just let it be a variable.

00:05:01.472 --> 00:05:03.969
We create a C object and print c.f.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:07.644
Of course, C defines an f, so it should print C.

00:05:07.644 --> 00:05:10.315
But what if C didn't define its own f.

00:05:10.315 --> 00:05:14.584
Well, C inherits&nbsp;from both A and B. And A and B both do define f.

00:05:14.584 --> 00:05:18.966
So, when we look up c.f, we should be able to find&nbsp;one of these f's.

00:05:18.966 --> 00:05:21.280
But which one should we choose?

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.880
And if neither A nor B defined f, we should still&nbsp;be able to find it all the way up at the Root.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.320
By now, I hope you can see&nbsp;that this is a search problem.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.912
Given a class, when I try to look&nbsp;up an attribute,

00:05:32.912 --> 00:05:36.392
I need to decide on some order to look through that class

00:05:36.392 --> 00:05:39.867
and its&nbsp;parents and their parents and so on and so forth.

00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:43.370
This is what the Method Resolution Order for a&nbsp;class does.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.718
Here, the full class representations are kind of long.

00:05:45.718 --> 00:05:49.124
So, I'm also just printing out&nbsp;the names of the classes in the MRO.

00:05:49.124 --> 00:05:54.299
So, as you can see, the MRO for the class C is C, A, B, Root, object.

00:05:54.299 --> 00:05:56.308
That means, when you try to look up a value in C,

00:05:56.308 --> 00:06:00.798
it will first check in C, then in A, then in B, then&nbsp;in Root, then in object.

00:06:00.798 --> 00:06:06.188
If Python searches through all of these and still can't find your attribute,&nbsp;then an attribute error is raised.

00:06:06.188 --> 00:06:08.633
Hmm. C, A, B, Root.

00:06:08.633 --> 00:06:13.281
That's exactly the order we saw when we were doing&nbsp;printouts with super calls.

00:06:13.281 --> 00:06:18.224
Remember this example. We saw C.f then A.f then B.f then Root.f

00:06:18.224 --> 00:06:22.240
So,&nbsp;finally we can understand where super is taking us.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:26.314
A super call does not take you to the object's&nbsp;parent class.

00:06:26.314 --> 00:06:32.105
It takes you to the next thing after the current class in the object's MRO.

00:06:32.105 --> 00:06:36.939
The way&nbsp;you should read and think about super in your head is next in line.

00:06:36.939 --> 00:06:39.749
super means next in line.

00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:42.599
In a single inheritance case that line is simple.

00:06:42.599 --> 00:06:45.320
Next in line is your parent.

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:48.720
But with multiple&nbsp;inheritance, that's not necessarily the case.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.226
As a programmer, there are just a few different&nbsp;properties of the MRO that you need to keep in mind.

00:06:53.226 --> 00:06:57.565
The MRO of any class starts with the class&nbsp;and eventually ends in object.

00:06:57.565 --> 00:07:01.138
Because all Python objects eventually inherit from object.

00:07:01.138 --> 00:07:04.015
The next&nbsp;property is that a child goes before its parents

00:07:04.015 --> 00:07:09.613
and the parents must maintain their relative order&nbsp;as they appear in the declaration of the child.

00:07:09.613 --> 00:07:12.850
So, C goes before A and A goes before B.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.640
In&nbsp;this case, the resulting MRO is C, A, B, object.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.799
The third important property is that the&nbsp;MRO of a child must be an extension of the MRO of each of its parents.

00:07:22.799 --> 00:07:26.800
That means each&nbsp;parent's MRO is a subsequence of the child's.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:32.953
This property ensures that an object's parents&nbsp;and their parents and their parents and so on all appear in the MRO.

00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:35.441
And the last important&nbsp;property of the MRO is that

00:07:35.441 --> 00:07:39.885
if these rules create a contradiction, then you'll get an error when you&nbsp;try to define the class.

00:07:39.885 --> 00:07:44.245
We'll get an error trying to create this D class because A is supposed&nbsp;to come before C.

00:07:44.245 --> 00:07:46.950
But C is supposed to come before A.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:52.000
That's a contradiction. So, we get an error&nbsp;telling us that we can't create a consistent MRO.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.101
But in the real world, you don't need to use logic&nbsp;to figure out the MRO

00:07:55.101 --> 00:07:57.744
just access the dunder MRO attribute.

00:07:57.744 --> 00:08:00.042
Still though, even if you understand MRO

00:08:00.042 --> 00:08:04.258
and you understand that super means go to the next thing in the MRO and call that,

00:08:04.258 --> 00:08:07.318
this still presents&nbsp;an interesting design challenge.

00:08:07.318 --> 00:08:13.584
When I design a class, I can't possibly imagine every single&nbsp;possible class that could be the next thing in the MRO.

00:08:13.584 --> 00:08:15.855
This poses two major issues.

00:08:15.855 --> 00:08:20.159
First: If a super&nbsp;call isn't necessarily going to call my parents version of the function,

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:24.716
then how do I ensure that&nbsp;my parents version of the function is eventually called?

00:08:24.716 --> 00:08:29.545
Presumably, I need that functionality. That's&nbsp;why I'm inheriting from my parent.

00:08:29.545 --> 00:08:33.227
And secondly, since a super call can go basically anywhere,

00:08:33.227 --> 00:08:36.575
how&nbsp;do I know that the parameters that I'm passing to that call are correct?

00:08:36.575 --> 00:08:41.366
For instance, what if the&nbsp;next class in line needs an x parameter that I don't have to give.

00:08:41.366 --> 00:08:46.080
In general, it's not possible to&nbsp;make super work with an arbitrary class hierarchy.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.536
Instead, the solution is cooperative inheritance.

00:08:49.536 --> 00:08:53.045
The only way you can make this work is if through some means you can ensure that

00:08:53.045 --> 00:08:56.464
all classes&nbsp;in the hierarchy obey certain rules.

00:08:56.464 --> 00:09:01.840
The first rule is that there should be some Root class&nbsp;that everything in the hierarchy inherits from.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.563
An explicit Root class is often the easiest&nbsp;to work with.

00:09:04.563 --> 00:09:09.233
But it's also okay if everything ultimately inherits from a built-in like set&nbsp;or dict.

00:09:09.233 --> 00:09:12.869
The second rule is that if you use a super call in one version of a method,

00:09:12.869 --> 00:09:17.450
then you&nbsp;should use the super call in every version of the method in the whole hierarchy

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:19.189
except, possibly,&nbsp;the Root.

00:09:19.189 --> 00:09:25.371
This is what will ensure that a super call will eventually result in your parent&nbsp;classes version of the function being called.

00:09:25.371 --> 00:09:27.824
The parent class might not be the next in line.

00:09:27.824 --> 00:09:32.513
But whatever is will also make a super call and then whatever's next will also make a super call

00:09:32.513 --> 00:09:35.032
and eventually it will arrive at your parent.

00:09:35.032 --> 00:09:40.376
The Root object versions of that method will then act&nbsp;as a sink to end the chain of super calls.

00:09:40.376 --> 00:09:44.821
Now, let's see how we make sure the arguments that we pass to&nbsp;a super call make sense.

00:09:44.821 --> 00:09:48.898
The first solution is one we've already seen and it's the one that dict uses.

00:09:48.898 --> 00:09:52.972
Just make all of the versions of the function take the exact same arguments.

00:09:52.972 --> 00:09:56.070
This is a great first&nbsp;choice but it's also very restrictive.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.209
Consider an init method.

00:09:58.209 --> 00:10:00.441
If I have a whole hierarchy of&nbsp;classes,

00:10:00.441 --> 00:10:04.513
I can't very well expect them to take the same parameters in their init.

00:10:04.513 --> 00:10:08.480
In this case,&nbsp;I recommend what I call keyword argument peeling.

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.409
Do you know what signature can match any function?

00:10:11.409 --> 00:10:13.785
*args **kwargs

00:10:13.785 --> 00:10:17.554
Make your function take any positional arguments and any keyword&nbsp;arguments.

00:10:17.554 --> 00:10:21.117
And then just peel off the keyword arguments that you actually want.

00:10:21.117 --> 00:10:23.372
In this case, I'm&nbsp;defining a ValidatedSet.

00:10:23.372 --> 00:10:28.133
It's just a set. But when you try to add an element into it, it checks that&nbsp;the element is valid.

00:10:28.133 --> 00:10:31.605
The initializer takes in an optional list of validators.

00:10:31.605 --> 00:10:34.589
It doesn't matter what&nbsp;other keyword arguments you pass to this function.

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:40.733
Anything that's not validators gets shoved&nbsp;into kwargs which is ignored and then passed on through the super call.

00:10:40.733 --> 00:10:42.931
If every class in the&nbsp;hierarchy cooperates,

00:10:42.931 --> 00:10:49.159
this will eventually pass all the unused args and kwargs to the Root object&nbsp;which in this case is set.

00:10:49.159 --> 00:10:51.862
set will of course eat all the remaining positional arguments

00:10:51.862 --> 00:10:55.032
and error&nbsp;if there are any remaining keyword arguments.

00:10:55.032 --> 00:10:57.080
For the add method, we take the simpler approach.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:00.022
Every add method should take one positional argument.

00:11:00.022 --> 00:11:01.923
We can then use the validated set like&nbsp;this.

00:11:01.923 --> 00:11:07.157
We pass in a list of validators in this case I'm just checking that all the elements&nbsp;are integers.

00:11:07.157 --> 00:11:10.345
But trying to add the string "5" results in an error.

00:11:10.345 --> 00:11:13.775
Let's see another example.&nbsp;This time I'll have a ReducedSet.

00:11:13.775 --> 00:11:16.372
Whenever you try to add something into a ReducedSet,

00:11:16.372 --> 00:11:19.600
it first&nbsp;reduces the element before adding it into the set.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:25.986
Again we take *args, **kwargs and an&nbsp;optional function to reduce the elements.

00:11:25.986 --> 00:11:30.854
This time when we try to add an element, we first reduce&nbsp;it before passing it along through a super call.

00:11:30.854 --> 00:11:35.475
Then we can create a modular set which is both&nbsp;a validated set and a reduced set.

00:11:35.475 --> 00:11:39.738
I'm thinking about a modular set as being like integers mod n.

00:11:39.738 --> 00:11:43.974
So, I pass on the validators list as being just a single list of is_int.

00:11:43.974 --> 00:11:47.840
And I take the reducer&nbsp;to be a function which reduces things mod_n.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.572
We can then take a set of integers mod5.

00:11:50.572 --> 00:11:54.791
5&nbsp;and 10 are multiples of 5. So, they'll wrap around and be reduced to 0.

00:11:54.791 --> 00:11:58.000
So, when we&nbsp;run the example, we just see 0, 1 and 2.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.523
And if we try to add something that's not an&nbsp;int, we get an error.

00:12:01.523 --> 00:12:07.989
At this point, I hope we understand a little bit about how to use super in&nbsp;both single and multiple inheritance settings.

00:12:07.989 --> 00:12:12.621
But have you ever thought about what super is actually&nbsp;doing?

00:12:12.621 --> 00:12:15.760
There's clearly some weird behavior going on.

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.520
First off, I can't even use the zero argument super&nbsp;outside of a class. I just get a runtime error.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:27.291
Okay. So, let's create A. And B that inherits from A&nbsp;and they both have functions f. And B calls super f

00:12:27.291 --> 00:12:30.861
Let's try extracting super into a variable&nbsp;and then using it.

00:12:30.861 --> 00:12:32.806
Okay, it still works if we do that.

00:12:32.806 --> 00:12:36.050
Quiz time. What is this super variable?

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.921
Take&nbsp;a moment to think about it.

00:12:37.921 --> 00:12:41.702
People often think that super returns the parent class object.

00:12:41.702 --> 00:12:46.610
Or&nbsp;if you're more enlightened, maybe you would say that it returns the next in line class object.

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.440
But this is not what super actually returns.

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:55.469
The return value of a super call is an&nbsp;instance of the super class.

00:12:55.469 --> 00:13:01.546
That's right. Super is just a class and a super call is just&nbsp;constructing an instance of that class.

00:13:01.546 --> 00:13:04.503
An instance of super is what's called a proxy object.

00:13:04.503 --> 00:13:10.603
It's&nbsp;a wrapper class that stores an object and then forwards attribute lookups to that object.

00:13:10.603 --> 00:13:13.760
Here's&nbsp;an example of the most basic kind of proxy object.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.440
I literally take in an object&nbsp;to the constructor and store it.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:22.560
The getattr function is what's called whenever&nbsp;you use dot something on an instance of a class.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.881
So, if I called proxy.abc, then the string abc would&nbsp;be passed to this function.

00:13:27.881 --> 00:13:32.892
We then use the built in getattr to pass that same request onto the&nbsp;stored object and return it.

00:13:32.892 --> 00:13:36.756
We could then use it like this. Say, I create an object, a list [1, 2, 3]

00:13:36.756 --> 00:13:38.775
I create a proxy for that object

00:13:38.775 --> 00:13:41.160
and then I say proxy.append(4)

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.706
proxy.append will call the&nbsp;getattr

00:13:43.706 --> 00:13:47.647
and the getattr will then return the underlying lists append function.

00:13:47.647 --> 00:13:51.600
We then call it&nbsp;with 4 and 4 gets appended to the underlying list.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.304
But super doesn't just forward attribute requests&nbsp;and return the result.

00:13:55.304 --> 00:13:57.257
It does something a little bit different.

00:13:57.257 --> 00:14:01.559
Here's a proxy object that's a&nbsp;little bit more similar to what super actually does.

00:14:01.559 --> 00:14:05.662
This proxy object takes in a class and an&nbsp;object and it stores them.

00:14:05.662 --> 00:14:09.100
Then to look up an item, it forwards it to the class object.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:12.480
If the result&nbsp;is something like a function that has a get method,

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:18.480
then it calls that get method to bind, say, the&nbsp;function to that object and return the function.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:23.817
Now, we can use our kinda super proxy object kind&nbsp;of like we use super.

00:14:23.817 --> 00:14:28.661
Then when we run it, we see the same B A printout that we would if we used&nbsp;super.

00:14:28.661 --> 00:14:32.914
But there's one big difference here between this and the real super.

00:14:32.914 --> 00:14:37.200
Here, I'm just passing&nbsp;in A, telling it where the super call should go.

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:41.328
But with the real super we didn't pass any&nbsp;arguments at all.

00:14:41.328 --> 00:14:44.148
So, how can it possibly know what class is being run from,

00:14:44.148 --> 00:14:48.394
what the&nbsp;self object is, or where the super call should go next?

00:14:48.394 --> 00:14:52.640
Hold on to your shoes because&nbsp;super is doing something pretty cursed here.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:57.659
Did you know that in Python you can get access to&nbsp;the currently executing stack frame?

00:14:57.659 --> 00:14:59.627
Let's take the current frame,

00:14:59.627 --> 00:15:02.221
go one function up in the stack

00:15:02.221 --> 00:15:05.680
and&nbsp;look at what are the local variables in that scope

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:11.183
This allows us to look up the local variables&nbsp;of the function that called this function.

00:15:11.183 --> 00:15:17.360
Take a look. We have x equals five, s equals "subscribe" and then we call this print_callers_locals.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:22.801
As you can see, print_callers_locals was able&nbsp;to see the x equals five and s equals "subscribe".

00:15:22.801 --> 00:15:28.160
Okay, let's create a class and a method and&nbsp;call print_callers_locals inside the method.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:33.760
We didn't pass any parameters to print_callers_locals but clearly it can see the self variable.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.475
But don't worry. It gets even more cursed.

00:15:37.475 --> 00:15:39.763
super can use this trick to find the self parameter

00:15:39.763 --> 00:15:43.258
or actually what it does is&nbsp;look at the first positional argument.

00:15:43.258 --> 00:15:48.161
But where does it get the information&nbsp;about where this code is being run from?

00:15:48.161 --> 00:15:53.344
Well, watch what happens if I so much as&nbsp;mention the word super inside the function.

00:15:55.511 --> 00:15:58.762
I didn't even call it. I just mentioned the word&nbsp;super.

00:15:58.762 --> 00:16:02.494
And now we have a new local variable in our function.

00:16:02.494 --> 00:16:04.243
dunder class

00:16:04.243 --> 00:16:08.024
That's right. If you&nbsp;so much as mention the word super inside of a function,

00:16:08.024 --> 00:16:14.599
then Python will add this secret dunder&nbsp;class variable that contains the class that is currently executing.

00:16:14.599 --> 00:16:17.420
The same thing happens if&nbsp;you just mention dunder class.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:24.152
So now, a zero argument super can gather all the information&nbsp;that it needs by looking in its caller's stack frame.

00:16:24.152 --> 00:16:27.321
This is super's true form.

00:16:27.321 --> 00:16:30.580
What's actually&nbsp;happening with the zero argument form of super is

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:34.861
it's sneakily going up the stack&nbsp;frame and grabbing these two variables.

00:16:34.861 --> 00:16:38.720
The two argument form of super is&nbsp;how you should really think about it.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.614
It's a proxy object that stores the class that&nbsp;it's currently being run from

00:16:42.614 --> 00:16:45.620
and the object that it's currently being run on.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:49.179
Then it uses&nbsp;the MRO to find the next thing in line.

00:16:49.179 --> 00:16:53.480
It's just compiler magic that this works if you don't&nbsp;pass in the arguments.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.057
You can actually use this two argument form of super if you want

00:16:56.057 --> 00:16:59.360
and you can&nbsp;actually use it anywhere even outside of a class.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:04.400
You just tell it what class it's supposed to&nbsp;pretend it's running from and what object to proxy.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.158
I create a B object,

00:17:06.158 --> 00:17:11.332
create a super that's supposed&nbsp;to be running from within B on a little instance b.

00:17:11.332 --> 00:17:16.960
Then when I go to look up the method f, I&nbsp;get the method from A bound to the B object.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:20.480
Then you could call it just like&nbsp;you would any other super call.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.028
If you want to see the information a super object&nbsp;has stored,

00:17:23.028 --> 00:17:27.098
you can access the self, self_class and thisclass attributes.

00:17:27.098 --> 00:17:31.040
self is going to be the&nbsp;object little b that it's supposed to be proxying.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:36.412
thisclass is the first argument, big B where&nbsp;it's supposed to be pretending to run the code from that class.

00:17:36.412 --> 00:17:42.597
self_class is used in order to&nbsp;make super work both with B instances and if you pass in the B class.

00:17:42.597 --> 00:17:47.040
If I pass in the b class, like&nbsp;what would happen if I was using a class method,

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.760
then self_class will be the class itself rather&nbsp;than the type of that class which would be type.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:57.529
These can actually all three be different&nbsp;typically if your super call is the result of another super call.

00:17:57.529 --> 00:18:02.181
That would cause the class&nbsp;that the code is being run from to be different from the type of the object.

00:18:02.181 --> 00:18:07.964
There's also a single&nbsp;argument form of super where you just pass the class and don't pass the object to bind.

00:18:07.964 --> 00:18:11.201
The only&nbsp;thing useful you can do with a super that hasn't been bound to an object

00:18:11.201 --> 00:18:14.445
is to bind it to an object&nbsp;at some later time.

00:18:14.445 --> 00:18:17.730
But in my experience, I have never needed to do this.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:22.560
So finally, are you ready&nbsp;to see my pure Python implementation of super?

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:25.909
It's not for the faint of heart. So, thank you&nbsp;if you want to see it.

00:18:25.909 --> 00:18:29.238
It's also probably full of bugs but it does work.

00:18:29.238 --> 00:18:32.800
First off, in init,&nbsp;we take the class and the object to proxy.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:37.360
The most interesting case is when both of&nbsp;these are None. That's the zero argument super.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:42.720
In that case, we get the current stack frame,&nbsp;look up the local variables of our caller

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.640
and find the first positional argument&nbsp;which is probably going to be self.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.551
Then we look up the special dunder class variable.

00:18:49.551 --> 00:18:52.480
Now, this is one exception that I do have to make.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:54.379
The real super gets special treatment.

00:18:54.379 --> 00:18:55.857
In&nbsp;that, if you even mention its name,

00:18:55.857 --> 00:19:00.695
Python will automatically fill in this underclass variable in&nbsp;the caller's local variables.

00:19:00.695 --> 00:19:03.031
Well, I don't get that special treatment.

00:19:03.031 --> 00:19:05.764
So, what I'm going to say is if&nbsp;you want to use my super,

00:19:05.764 --> 00:19:09.537
then you have to mention dunder class somewhere in your function.

00:19:09.537 --> 00:19:13.822
Either&nbsp;do that or use the two argument form. That's the only exception that I'm making.

00:19:13.822 --> 00:19:16.379
So, I try to look&nbsp;up the dunder class variable

00:19:16.379 --> 00:19:18.000
then we set our this class variable

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:23.067
and then use this bind_self&nbsp;method to set our self and self_class variables.

00:19:23.067 --> 00:19:27.719
This case is for the one argument form and I just&nbsp;said self and self_class to None.

00:19:27.719 --> 00:19:30.618
Otherwise, we check if our object is itself a class.

00:19:30.618 --> 00:19:33.680
In that&nbsp;case, both self and self_class are that class.

00:19:33.680 --> 00:19:37.141
Otherwise, the object better be an instance of the&nbsp;given class.

00:19:37.141 --> 00:19:41.512
In which case, the self is our object and the self_class is its type.

00:19:41.512 --> 00:19:45.026
Let's ignore get.&nbsp;That's just to make the single argument version of super work

00:19:45.026 --> 00:19:47.472
which you shouldn't even be using&nbsp;anyway.

00:19:47.472 --> 00:19:52.398
Then we get to the meat of super, the getattr the thing that actually does the proxying.

00:19:52.398 --> 00:19:54.887
We start by looking up the MRO.

00:19:54.887 --> 00:19:58.952
And then take the next index after thisclass in the MRO.

00:19:58.952 --> 00:20:01.767
This is&nbsp;the next in line part of super.

00:20:01.767 --> 00:20:05.686
We look up this class in the MRO and then go to the next index.

00:20:05.686 --> 00:20:08.720
Then we start looping over the rest of the MRO.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:10.950
We grab out the ith class

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:14.160
then we try to&nbsp;look up the item in that class's dictionary

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:18.356
If the lookup fails, then we just pass and go to&nbsp;the next iteration.

00:20:18.356 --> 00:20:23.136
Otherwise, we check to see if the thing that we got back has a get method like a&nbsp;function.

00:20:23.136 --> 00:20:27.906
And if it does have a get method, then we need to bind the function to the correct argument.

00:20:27.906 --> 00:20:30.450
After we bind the instance, then we return it.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:35.760
If we look through the entire MRO and still didn't&nbsp;find it, then we just raise an attribute error.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.885
And here's how you can use it. I just have&nbsp;a simple class hierarchy.

00:20:38.885 --> 00:20:44.080
I just mentioned dunder class and then I use super with zero&nbsp;arguments just like I would the normal super.

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:48.480
Well, that's all I've got. Thank you so much&nbsp;for watching to the end if you got this far.

